The one real fork: full-text search matches whole words (stemmed), so a typo or a partial word ("lapt") won't hit. If the product asks for "search as you type" or typo-tolerance, layer in `pg_trgm` (trigram GIN index + `similarity()`/`%` operator) alongside `tsvector`, or combine both in one query. Plain word-based search covers most "type a few words" cases and is the cheaper build — I'd ship that first and add trigram only if user testing shows people expect fuzzy matches.

Left out: query debouncing/pagination UX, ranking tuning (weights, `ts_rank_cd`), and reindex cost on writes (negligible at a few hundred rows/month).

On this platform, real push is off the table: `PLATFORM.md` says WebSocket upgrades 501 at the edge, functions die at 10 seconds, and there's no sticky routing or shared memory to hold a stream open across invocations even if one *did* survive. So SSE/long-polling don't work here either — they'd need a held-open connection this platform can't provide.

What's left, and what's actually sufficient: client-side polling of `/api/metrics` on a timer, short-circuiting the render with `computed_at` so a poll that returns the same rollup (true 59 minutes out of 60) doesn't redraw the DOM.

```js
// public/dashboard.js
let lastComputedAt = null;

async function load() {
  const res = await fetch('/api/metrics');
  const { rows, computed_at } = await res.json();
  if (computed_at === lastComputedAt) return;
  lastComputedAt = computed_at;
  render(rows);
  document.getElementById('stamp').textContent = new Date(computed_at).toLocaleTimeString();
}

document.getElementById('refresh').addEventListener('click', load);
window.addEventListener('load', load);
setInterval(load, 15000);
```

The one real decision is cadence: `rollup.md` says the table changes once a minute, on the minute, so polling faster than that only re-fetches byte-identical rows — 15-20s gets the update visible within a bounded delay without hammering `metrics.js`. Left out: pausing the interval on `document.visibilityState` for backgrounded tabs, and backoff on fetch failure — worth adding but not the core mechanism.

Every write in this service already funnels through one chokepoint — `write()` in db.js:13-26. That's the place to hook this, not each route handler.

**The fork:** log from the application (add an insert inside `write()`) or log from the database (a trigger on each audited table). App-layer is simpler and has `req.user` for free, but it's bypassable — any future code that calls `pool.query` directly instead of `db.write` skips the trail, and capturing "before" values means an extra `SELECT` before every `UPDATE`/`DELETE`. A DB trigger can't be bypassed and gets the before/after row automatically via `OLD`/`NEW`, at the cost of needing the actor identity threaded into the session since triggers can't see `req.user`.

Recommend the trigger, with actor passed in via a session variable set at the top of `write()`:

```sql
CREATE TABLE audit_log (
  id          bigserial PRIMARY KEY,
  table_name  text NOT NULL,
  row_id      text NOT NULL,
  action      text NOT NULL,          -- INSERT / UPDATE / DELETE
  actor_id    text,                   -- from app.user_id session var
  old_row     jsonb,
  new_row     jsonb,
  changed_at  timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now()
);

CREATE FUNCTION audit_row() RETURNS trigger AS $$
BEGIN
  INSERT INTO audit_log(table_name, row_id, action, actor_id, old_row, new_row)
  VALUES (
    TG_TABLE_NAME,
    COALESCE(NEW.id, OLD.id)::text,
    TG_OP,
    current_setting('app.user_id', true),
    CASE WHEN TG_OP <> 'INSERT' THEN to_jsonb(OLD) END,
    CASE WHEN TG_OP <> 'DELETE' THEN to_jsonb(NEW) END
  );
  RETURN NEW;
END;
$$ LANGUAGE plpgsql;

CREATE TRIGGER accounts_audit AFTER INSERT OR UPDATE OR DELETE ON accounts
  FOR EACH ROW EXECUTE FUNCTION audit_row();
-- same on orders, and any other table compliance cares about
```

