The one fork worth deciding now: whether "matching" needs typo tolerance (`pg_trgm` + trigram similarity, stacked on top of the same GIN index) or exact-word stemmed matching is good enough for launch — the latter is a no-op to add later since it's additive, so I'd ship without it and add only if support tickets show people mistyping product names.

At 38k rows on Postgres, this doesn't need a separate search engine — Postgres full-text search on the existing table is the right call.

Add a generated `tsvector` column combining name and description with name weighted higher, index it with GIN, and query via `websearch_to_tsquery` ordered by `ts_rank`:

```sql
ALTER TABLE products ADD COLUMN search_vector tsvector
  GENERATED ALWAYS AS (
    setweight(to_tsvector('english', name), 'A') ||
    setweight(to_tsvector('english', description), 'B')
  ) STORED;

CREATE INDEX products_search_idx ON products USING GIN (search_vector);
```

```js
router.get("/search", async (req, res) => {
  const q = (req.query.q || "").trim();
  if (!q) return res.json([]);
  const rows = await db.query(
    `SELECT id, sku, name, price_cents,
            ts_rank(search_vector, websearch_to_tsquery('english', $1)) AS rank
     FROM products
     WHERE active AND search_vector @@ websearch_to_tsquery('english', $1)
     ORDER BY rank DESC LIMIT 50`,
    [q]
  );
  res.json(rows);
});
```

The one real fork: plain `tsvector` matches whole words only, so "lapto" won't find "laptop" and typos return nothing. If the product owner wants prefix/typo tolerance, add `pg_trgm` with a GIN trigram index and blend it with the tsvector query (or switch to trigram-only similarity matching for short queries). Worth deciding upfront since it changes the index and query shape, not just a tuning knob added later.

Left out: relevance tuning beyond the A/B weighting, and whether search needs to survive a jump past ~1M rows (that's when Elasticsearch/Typesense would earn their keep).
